# 2. Creating the Express application

**Lines 13–18:**

```js
//Creates the express application
const app = express();
app.use(cors());
//tells express if the browser sends a JSON parse it and put it in req.body
app.use(express.json({ limit: "5mb" }));
// Tale all routes defined by canvas-routes.js and attach them underneath /api/canvas
app.use("/api/canvas", canvasRoutes);
```

### Creating the server

```js
const app = express();
```

Creates the Express application.

The `app` object is then used to configure middleware and HTTP endpoints.

### Enabling CORS

```js
app.use(cors());
```

Enables CORS for the server.

This allows browser requests from other origins to reach the Express server.

### Parsing JSON requests

```js
app.use(express.json({ limit: "5mb" }));
```

Tells Express to automatically parse JSON request bodies.

For example, if LawGPT sends:

```json
{
  "model": "some-model",
  "input": "Hello"
}
```

Express makes the data available through:

```js
req.body
```

The `5mb` limit allows requests with bodies up to approximately 5 megabytes.

### Registering Canvas routes

```js
app.use("/api/canvas", canvasRoutes);
```

Mounts all routes defined in `canvas-routes.js` underneath `/api/canvas`.

For example, if `canvas-routes.js` contains:

```js
router.post("/save", ...)
```

the resulting endpoint would be:

```text
/api/canvas/save
```

