# 11. Sending the request to OpenAI

**Lines 106–115:**

```js
const openaiRes = await fetch(OPENAI_URL, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    "Authorization": "Bearer " + apiKey
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ model, input })
});
```

This is where the proxy makes the actual request to OpenAI.

The request is sent to:

```text
https://api.openai.com/v1/responses
```

The important part is the authorization header:

```js
"Authorization": "Bearer " + apiKey
```

The server adds the API key to the request.

Conceptually, the request looks like:

```text
POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses

Authorization: Bearer sk-...
Content-Type: application/json
```

The browser does not have to construct this authorization header itself.

The request body is created with:

```js
JSON.stringify({ model, input })
```

which converts the JavaScript object into JSON before sending it to OpenAI.

---

# 12. Returning OpenAI's response to LawGPT

**Lines 117–118:**

```js
const data = await openaiRes.json();
res.status(openaiRes.status).json(data);
```

After OpenAI responds, the server parses the response as JSON.

It then sends that JSON back to LawGPT.

The server also preserves OpenAI's HTTP status code:

```js
res.status(openaiRes.status)
```

For example, if OpenAI returns an error status, the proxy passes that status back to LawGPT rather than automatically returning `200 OK`.

The complete request flow is therefore:

```text
LawGPT
   │
   │ POST /api/chat
   │ { model, input }
   ▼
Express Server
   │
   │ Adds API key
   │
   │ POST /v1/responses
   ▼
OpenAI
   │
   │ JSON response
   ▼
Express Server
   │
   │ Same status + JSON
   ▼
LawGPT
```

