# 16. Why LawGPT uses a proxy

The most important architectural feature of this file is that LawGPT does **not** send its OpenAI API request directly from the browser.

Instead, the browser communicates with this local Express server.

### Without the proxy

The architecture would look like:

```text
LawGPT Browser
      │
      │ API key would have to be available here
      ▼
OpenAI API
```

That would require the frontend to have access to the API credential.

### With this proxy

The architecture is:

```text
┌─────────────────────┐
│      LawGPT UI      │
│      Browser        │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           │ POST /api/chat
           │ model + input
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│    Express Server   │
│     server.js       │
│                     │
│     apiKey          │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           │ POST /v1/responses
           │ Authorization: Bearer <key>
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│      OpenAI API     │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           │ JSON response
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│    Express Server   │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           │ JSON response
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│      LawGPT UI      │
└─────────────────────┘
```

The proxy therefore acts as an intermediary between LawGPT and OpenAI.

